// =============================================================================================
// Error rate — the query, and the alert built on it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY A RATE AND NOT A COUNT
//
// A count threshold is wrong in both directions at once. Ten failures a minute is a catastrophe
// for a service doing twenty requests a minute and noise for one doing fifty thousand. A count
// alert has to be retuned every time traffic changes, and in practice it is not — so it either
// screams through a healthy traffic spike or stays silent through an outage that happens
// overnight when volume is low.
//
// A rate is dimensionless. Five percent means the same thing at any traffic level.
// =============================================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 1 — error rate over time, for a dashboard.
//
// Bucketed at 5 minutes because that is roughly how long a person waits before deciding
// something is wrong. Finer buckets are noisier without being more actionable.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
requests
| where timestamp > ago(1h)
| where cloud_RoleName == 'quotes-api'
| summarize
    total  = count(),
    failed = countif(success == false)
  by bin(timestamp, 5m)
| extend errorRatePct = round(100.0 * failed / total, 2)
| project timestamp, total, failed, errorRatePct
| order by timestamp asc


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 2 — which endpoints and which status codes.
//
// A rate says something is wrong. This says what. Run it second, not first.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// requests
// | where timestamp > ago(1h)
// | where success == false
// | summarize failures = count() by endpoint = name, resultCode
// | order by failures desc


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PART 3 — THE ALERT QUERY, exactly as deployed in infra/modules/alert.bicep.
//
// No time filter and no bin(). Both are deliberate: a scheduled query rule supplies its own
// window via `windowSize`, and adding `ago()` here would AND two windows together and quietly
// evaluate the wrong span of time.
//
// `| where Total > 10` is the line that matters most. Without it a single failed request in an
// idle minute is a 100% error rate and the alert fires — at 3am, on a dev environment, on a
// health probe. That one line is the difference between an alert people act on and an alert
// people mute, and a muted alert is worse than none because it still looks like coverage.
//
// `success == false` rather than `resultCode >= 500`, because a 4xx storm is also an outage from
// the caller's point of view. An expired key returning 401 to every client is not "fine, those
// are client errors".
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// requests
// | summarize Total = count(), Failed = countif(success == false)
// | where Total > 10
// | project ErrorRatePercent = round(100.0 * Failed / Total, 2)
//
// Fires when ErrorRatePercent > 5, evaluated every 5 minutes over a 15-minute window.
